"{\"Integration\":[{\"id\":\"730366054\",\"title\":\"Can external systems interface with IMOS?\",\"text\":\"It is possible to set up interfaces between IMOS and external systems to send and receive data. IMOS can receive interface messages via the following channels: Messaging Service Listeners API For IMOS to receive data from external systems, the interface message will need to adhere to the respective interface specifications. If you would like to set these up, please reach out to Veson Nautical Support or Professional Services for assistance. &zwnj;\"},{\"id\":\"763658607\",\"title\":\"Does Veson Nautical support Robotic Process Automation (RPA) for its products?\",\"text\":\"Veson Nautical does not support RPA in any form for any of its products. Clients should refrain from attempting to create RPA user accounts in their databases.\"},{\"id\":\"774308729\",\"title\":\"FAQ - Why are TCTO voyages not included in the notifications for DA-Desk?\",\"text\":\"The reason being the fact that in OVTO/TCTO, the Operating Company is not the one to appoint nor nominate agents. Under exceptions, they need to have their own protective agent (who they pay on their account) therefore, they can add these ops types in the notifications. In other words in OVTO & TCTO the Operating (W) Company is not involved in the voyage operations, therefore they don't need to appoint agents. Configuration flag CFGOprTypesForPortschinfoDA controls the operation types to be included for DA-Desk notifications. The default flag values are TCOV& OVOV. To include other operation types, user needs to add them to the Value field. [image] Once the above field has been changed, the messaging service for the client needs to be restarted for the changes to apply.\"},{\"id\":\"424902851\",\"title\":\"Some Interface Messages are stuck in Pending status or are not showing up in the Interface Messages List. How can I fix this?\",\"text\":\"The Messaging Service can become inundated or flooded if several Scheduled Tasks or other processes (included import and export) are simultaneously running. This causes the bandwidth of the Messaging Service to be filled, leaving no room for new messages to import or export, and often creating a queue that does not push through. This can be often be resolved by restarting the Messaging Service. Users may also find it helpful to increase the Timeout threshold in the General Configuration tab of the Messaging Service Configuration.\"},{\"id\":\"761037027\",\"title\":\"Why are my imported invoices not exporting to external financial databases?\",\"text\":\"Imported invoices from one external database can fail to export from IMOS to another external database if the original import fails to succeed in its original import. Open Failed messages in the Interface Message List to view their issues. For instance, an invoice may fail to import successfully if there are missing items in the invoice XML or if the imported invoice is attempting to post to a closed voyage.\"},{\"id\":\"1837989930\",\"title\":\"Why can't I see the billinv as a table in the database?\",\"text\":\"billinv is a view and because of that it is not listed in the Tables folder of the IMOS database.\"},{\"id\":\"790037087\",\"title\":\"Why does Company Import XML not update existing records even though the status shows \\\"Successful\\\"?\",\"text\":\"To update an existing company record in the Address Book using the Company Import XML, the reference code has to be indicated in the <referenceCode> field tag. If this tag in the XML is left empty, the XML message will still be processed successfully but the record will not be updated. CFGEnableMultiRoleOnAddressBook CFGAddBookUniqueRefCode Result Y N IMOS will update the Reference Code based on the combination of Short Name and Company Type. N Y IMOS will fail an incoming message if the referenceCode is already in use by another Address record. Y Y IMOS will allow Address records with the same short name to have the same Reference Code.\"},{\"id\":\"326664218\",\"title\":\"Why does my invoice import fail even though my line items and total amount match?\",\"text\":\"Problem You may encounter the following errors when importing invoices into your database: autoinv error: Invoice amount does not match the sum of the line items. autoinv error: Invoice base equivalent does not match the sum of the line items. These errors appear when the sum of the individual items does not match the total of the invoice. If these totals do indeed match up, there may be too many decimal places entered for one or more of the line items. The invoiceImport functionality will also fail if any of the currency values in the XML possess more than 2 decimal places. This is a limitation of the Messaging Service that cannot be modified. Solution It is recommended to import XML files with line items that have 2 decimal places or fewer. If invoices are submitted via integration with a third-party solution, Veson Nautical urges you to work with your third-party vendor to make necessary changes.\"},{\"id\":\"927105796\",\"title\":\"Why do Market Index data not appear when the Interface Message List shows that Route data has been successfully imported?\",\"text\":\"This issue often occurs when there is an erroneous space placed in the <CMSRouteId/> line item(s) of the custom market data files unique to your organization, which you will need to correct. Incorrect ❌ Correct ✔️ <CMSRouteId>Route_Data123 +3MON</CMSRouteId> <CMSRouteId>Route_Data123+3MON</CMSRouteId>\"},{\"id\":\"4040720502\",\"title\":\"Why is a SimplePayment Message failing with error \\\"Unable to Get Exchange Rate Between <USD> and <>\\\"?\",\"text\":\"This error occurs when the system cannot determine the exchange rate due to missing or incomplete bank details in the address book record. The most common reasons for this issue are: Missing currency information in the bank setup Missing bank code or related mandatory fields Without this information, the system is unable to identify the target currency, resulting in a failed exchange rate lookup. To fix the issue: Navigate to the Data Center module &rarr; Address book record used in the payment. Check the bank setup section. Ensure that: A valid currency (e.g., USD) is specified. A valid bank code is entered. [image] Save the updates. Once the correct bank details are in place, resend the SimplePayment message.\"}],\"Sustainability\":[{\"id\":\"3286597735\",\"title\":\"How is the Energy Penalty constant calculated for FuelEU?\",\"text\":\"The FuelEU Maritime penalty uses two variables in its calculation. &euro;2,400 per tonne of very low sulfur fuel oil (VLSFO) equivalent energy that exceeds the limit. 41,000 : This is a constant that represents the energy equivalent of 1 metric ton of Very Low Sulphur Fuel Oil (VLSFO), which is equivalent to 41,000 MJ. Energy Penalty = &euro;2,400 / 41,000 &asymp; 0.058\"},{\"id\":\"4564254772\",\"title\":\"Why is the Carbon/Emission Allowance Invoices a Journal?\",\"text\":\"Short Answer Carbon (Emissions) Allowance Invoice are intentionally implemented so that: The user sees and handles it similar to an \\\"invoice\\\" (invoices list, approvals, contract workflow). But the posted transaction in the accounting layer is a journal , not an AR/AP invoice. This was done because an Emissions Allowance Invoice is meant to create or adjust a balance‑sheet liability for allowances , not to create an open receivable/payable like a normal cash invoice. Why a journal and not a \\\"real\\\" invoice? 1. Nature of the transaction: liability, not cash A Carbon/Emissions Allowance Invoice is used to: Recognize that the company owes emissions allowances (or has consumed them) for a period. Record a liability on the balance sheet and related expense on the P&L. At that point: There is no external cash payment due , and The settlement of that obligation is done via Allowance Transfers . Because of this, the accounting event is: Balance‑sheet + P&L entry only (expense + allowance liability), No AR/AP open item that needs payment/receipt tracking. That is exactly what journals are for in IMOS/VIP. 2. Link to Allowance Transfers and closing the balance Per IMOS - Emissions Expense Settlement Workflow The flow is: Post Emissions Allowance Invoice Creates a journal to record the new liability. Later, perform Allowance Transfers Transfer journals then close or move that liability using Carbon Allowance inventory. If the Allowance Invoice had been implemented as a standard AR/AP invoice, you would end up with open payables/receivables that don't map cleanly to how EUA obligations are settled. Using journals keeps it strictly as liability and expense accounting tied to allowance inventory and transfers .\"},{\"id\":\"3500048389\",\"title\":\"Why is the energy calculated for bunkers in FUEL EU not as expected?\",\"text\":\"The energy value for bunkers is calculated as follows. Total Energy (MJ) = MT x 1,000,000 x LCV Where MT is the bunkers consumed in MT, and LCV is defined in Fuel/Lube Types [image] However, on a bunker lifting we can define these fuel factors specifically and these will override the defaults. (See https://vesonjira.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/help/pages/2958852152/IMOS+-+FuelEU+Maritime+Calculator#Capturing-Actual-Tested-Values-on-Voyage ) If Fuel Factors are not defined properly, this can lead to issues such as 0 MJ energy production. [image][image] Please ensure Fuel Factors are defined when linking Bunker Purchases to guarantee your Energy calculations are as expected.\"}],\"Time Charter\":[{\"id\":\"403865742\",\"title\":\"FAQ - Explanation of Bunkers on Consumption\",\"text\":\"Home > Time Charter FAQs > How do I schedule a Time Charter with Bunkers on Consumption basis? On the Bunkers tab of a Time Charter (TC), a Bunkers on Consumption check box appears only for TC Out contracts. Time Charter Out As the TC owner for TC Out contracts, you know the delivery and redelivery quantity and price. As a result, on the TC Out Bunkers tab, you can select Bunkers on Consumption to calculate delivery and redelivery adjustments. Check box cleared (by default) This can cause massive swings in the P&L if there are considerable price differences between the bunker inventory before the time charter out, and the bunker inventory after the TC Out voyage bunkers were redelivered at the contractual price. Check box selected When you select Bunker on Consumption, the bunkers consumed during the voyage calculate based on the bunker inventory prices. The only adjustment on the P&L will be based on the difference between the delivery and redelivery bunker quantities and pricing. (In order to not swing the P&L, the bunkers on consumption adjustment is only calculated once the Z port has been actualized).\"},{\"id\":\"730366163\",\"title\":\"FAQ - What is the calculation logic behind each CVE \\\"Rate Type\\\"?\",\"text\":\"When creating a contract, users can select from three Rate Types for Common Voyage Expenses (CVE) . The screenshot below shows the available options: Per 30 Days , Average Monthly , and Monthly . [image] And below you can find the methodology each \\\"Rate Type\\\" uses to calculate the final amount: Per 30 days Daily Rate Formula: Rate &divide; 30 Example: Rate = USD 1,500 Days in Hire = 31 Calculation: (1,500 &divide; 30) &times; 31 = USD 1,550 [image] Average Monthly Daily Rate Formula: Non-leap year: Rate &times; (12 &divide; 365) Leap year: Rate &times; (12 &divide; 366) Example 1 (31 days - non-leap year): Rate = USD 1,500 Days in Hire = 31 Calculation: 31 &times; [1,500 &times; (12 &divide; 365)] &asymp; USD 1,528.77 [image] Example 2 (31 days - leap year): Rate = USD 1,500 Days in Hire = 31 Calculation: 31 &times; [1,500 &times; (12 &divide; 366)] &asymp; USD 1,524.59 [image] Monthly Daily Rate Formula: Rate &divide; Actual Days in the Month e.g. March = 31 days, April = 30 days. Prorating Across Months - CFGAlwaysProrateMonthlyTCRates: The system's billing behavior depends on the configuration flag CFGAlwaysProrateMonthlyTCRates and whether the hire period exactly matches the number of days in a agiven month. When CFGAlwaysProrateMonthlyTCRates = N Exact Month Match If the invoice period covers exactly the number of days in that month, the system bills a full month at the contract rate. Example: Hire Period: 04 Feb 2023 00:00 GMT - 04 Mar 2023 00:00 GMT February 2023 has 28 days, matching the hire period. CVE Calculation: USD 1,500 (full monthly rate). [image] Partial Month (No Exact Match) If the invoice period does not match the number of days in the month, the system prorates the CVE across the months in the period. Example: Hire Period: 01 Feb 2023 00:00 GMT - 04 Mar 2023 00:00 GMT (31 days) February has 28 days, so the period exceeds the month. CVE Calculation: USD 1,645.16 , allocated as: (Rate &times; % of Month A) + (Rate &times; % of Month B) [image][image] When CFGAlwaysProrateMonthlyTCRates = Y The system always prorates the CVE amount based on the exact number of days from each month included in the invoice period&mdash; even if the hire period matches the full number of days in a month. Example: Hire Period: 04 Feb 2023 00:00 GMT - 04 Mar 2023 00:00 GMT February 2023 has 28 days, but with the flag set to Y , the system still prorates: [image][image] For Leap Years, the calculation can occasionally confuse users for the month of February. Please refer to the following article for illustration: https://vesonjira.atlassian.net/wiki/x/2YHT7g\"},{\"id\":\"428146765\",\"title\":\"How do I cancel a Time Charter contract?\",\"text\":\"To cancel a Time Charter (TC) contract, change the contract Status to Canceled . If changing the Status to Canceled causes issues, a voyage might be linked to the TC contract. Check the following before canceling a TC contract: Make sure all scheduled voyages linked to the TC have been deleted. A voyage with the Status Canceled must still be deleted prior to canceling its linked TC In/TC Out. It is good practice to cancel a TC Out prior to canceling a TC In.\"},{\"id\":\"98599980\",\"title\":\"How do I clear the Last TCI Voy check box?\",\"text\":\"Home > Time Charter FAQs > How do I clear the Last TCI Voy check box? For a TCOV or TCTO voyage, on the Voyage Manager : If the linked Time Charter contract is configured with Contract Type Trip TC , you cannot clear the Last TCI Voy check box. If you change the Contract Type to not be Trip TC, then you can clear the Last TCI Voy check box. The Last TCI Voy check box is selected by default for Trip TC in order to properly maintain the link between the last port of the voyage and the Time Charter redelivery.\"},{\"id\":\"71140190\",\"title\":\"How do I relink a Time Charter contract to a voyage?\",\"text\":\"Home > Time Charter FAQs > How do I relink a Time Charter contract to a voyage? About Time Charter Voyages For a Time Charter that is linked to a voyage , the delivery/redelivery dates and bunkers are driven by the voyage and automatically updated into the TC. When a Delivery/Redelivery Proj/Act GMT date is manually updated in the TC, the dates will appear in red font . This is an indication that the TC is now unlinked from the voyage. When this happens, any updates to the voyage will no longer update the TC automatically. [image] Relinking a TC to a Voyage Scenario 1 - Voyage Status: Completed, TC Status: Redelivered In the Time Charter : Clear all manual entries (shown in red font ) Update the TC Status from Redelivered to Delivered . Save and close the TC. In the Voyage Manager : Set the Voyage Status to Commenced . Save the voyage. Set the Voyage Status to Completed . Save the voyage. In the Time Charter , the Status updates to Redelivered , and Delivery / Redelivery dates automatically populate and appear in black font. Scenario 2 - Voyage Status: Commenced, TC Status: Delivered In the Time Charter : Clear all manual entries (shown in red font ). Update the TC Status from Delivered to Fixed . Save and close the TC. In the Voyage Manager , save the voyage. In the Time Charter , the Status updates to Delivered and the Delivery date populates and appears in black font.\"},{\"id\":\"64488596\",\"title\":\"How is TCO bunker adjustment calculated?\",\"text\":\"TCO bunker adjustment is considered in the revenue part of the P&L. The following variables are used in its calculation: VB1 = FIFO value of bunker grade (for example, IFO) in voyage at delivery TC1 = Value of bunker grade sold according to TCO terms VB2 = FIFO value of bunker grade in voyage at redelivery TC2 = Value of bunker grade purchased according to TCO terms TCO bunker adjustment is calculated in the system for the following scenarios: Scenario 1 : Bunker adjustment in a TCO voyage is calculated according to the difference between the FIFO inventory value of the voyage and the TCO delivery bunkers. The ending voyage bunkers of the TCO voyage will be priced at the TCO contract redelivery price. TCO bunker adjustment appears in the P&L when the vessel is delivered. TCO bunker adjustment = TC1 - VB1 Scenario 2 : Bunker adjustment in a TCO voyage is calculated according to the difference between the FIFO inventory value of the voyage and the TCO bunkers both on delivery and redelivery. This is activated with the Bunkers on Consumption checkbox. The ending voyage bunkers will be priced according to the FIFO voyage quantities and prices on delivery. TCO bunker adjustment appears in the P&L when the vessel is redelivered. In both cases, if there have been bunker purchases by the owner during the time charter voyage, these purchases will be treated separately according to FIFO. TCO bunker adjustment appears in the P&L when the vessel is delivered. TCO bunker adjustment = ( TC1 - VB1 ) + ( VB2 - TC2 )\"},{\"id\":\"5229773160\",\"title\":\"Logic behind calculated index-hire rates in Unpriced Component Rate Breakdown\",\"text\":\"Index-hire rates link the hire in a time charter to a market benchmark (e.g., Baltic Exchange routes), allowing both the owner and charterer to share market risk. If freight markets move significantly, neither party is heavily disadvantaged. The calculated daily index-hire rate depends on how the Unpriced Component Form is configured in the time charter. The system first calculates the adjusted market rate for each individual date in the averaging period. To determine which range's calculation should be followed to determine the final calculated hire rate, the system calculates the average of the current market rate first, and then compares it against the ranges available. The formula of determined range is then applied, giving the final index hire rate. An example is shown below: [image] Hire period: 06/14/2025 to 06/14/2026 General Correlation: 0.9865625000 Average period condition: Start from hire period start Time offset: -1 months, i.e., -1 months is added to the average period start (i.e., average period starts from 05/01/2025 instead of 06/01/25) Ave Period: 1 month, i.e., each average period will be one month long (e.g., 05/01/2025 to 05/31/2025) Fill days: None (no market rate for weekends/holidays/leaves) Range: Range Description Level Correlation Offset (, 12,500] For Adjusted Average Market rate smaller than and including 12500 12,500 0 12,500 (12,500, 15,000) For Adjusted Average Market rate between 12500 and 15000, excluding both 0 1 0 [15,000, ) For Adjusted Average Market rate bigger than and including 15000 15,000 0.3 15,000 Calculation method: Adjusted market rate for individual dates = Individual market rates * General correlation, i.e., Individual market rate * 0.9865625000 For example, for average period, 09/01/2025 to 09/30/2025, [image] Adjusted average market rate = Average market rate during the average period * General correlation For average period, 09/01/2025 to 09/30/2025 Avg market rate: 16,510.36 Adjusted avg rate: 16,510.36 &times; 0.9865625000 = 16,228.50 [image] Range determination : Since adjusted avg rate, 16,228.50 &ge; 15,000 , it falls in range [15,000, ), i.e., bigger than 15000 [image] Final index-hire rate calculation: Using the formula for range [15,000, ) , (Index price - Level) * (Correlation) + (Offset) final index-hire rate is calculated with Adjusted Market Rate for individual dates as the index price, as follows: (Adjusted market rate for individual dates - 15000) * (0.3) + (15000) [image]\"},{\"id\":\"747733059\",\"title\":\"Why am I unable to edit Bunker Breakdown in the Time Charter Out contract?\",\"text\":\"If a Time Charter Out contract has values in the Paid by Operator row, you will not be able to edit the Bunker Breakdown in the contract. [image] In order to edit the Bunker Breakdown, there are two alternatives: ALTERNATIVE 1 Edit all the bunker liftings that are \\\"Paid by Operator\\\" To find this list, you can go to the Voyage Manager, then go to \\\"Bunker Liftings\\\" (Bunkers > Bunker Liftings). [image] Take note of the original values of the \\\"For Account\\\" column. Edit the entries that have the value \\\"Operator\\\" under the \\\"Paid By\\\" column and change the \\\"For Account\\\" values for these identified rows. [image] Save the edits. Save the voyage. Go back to your TCO Contract, and unselect \\\"Bunker on Consumption\\\" in the Bunker section. [image] Select \\\"Bunker Breakdown\\\". You should now be able to edit the Redelivery Bunkers. Change the values to what is required. ALTERNATIVE 2 Navigate to the specific voyage and from the top menu select Voyage Bunkers &rarr; Bunker Liftings; change the For Account field from \\\"Operator\\\" to \\\"TCO Charterer\\\" &rarr; Save &rarr; Save the voyage and refresh. [image] Open the Time Charter Out contract &rarr; Bunkers tab &rarr; Bunker Breakdown &rarr; update the Redelivery grid price fields &rarr; Save. Navigate back to voyage Bunker Liftings screen and change back the For Account field to \\\"Operator\\\" &rarr; Save &rarr; Save voyage. [image] Navigate again to voyage Bunker Liftings screen within Voyage Manager and change back the For Account field to \\\"Operator\\\" &rarr; Save &rarr; Save voyage. Check the TCO contract again, and you will notice that there is a Supplement to Delivery row which you may delete it.\"},{\"id\":\"326598835\",\"title\":\"Why does my Time Charter contract's Proj/Act GMT Delivery field appear in red font?\",\"text\":\"Problem Delivery and Redelivery Proj/Act field values are pulled in from the linked voyage. Values that appear red are entered manually instead of being pulled in directly from the voyage. This can cause the Time Charter contract to be out of sync with the scheduled voyages. Solution Both the Delivery and Redelivery Proj/Act fields are reflected in red: In the Time charter (TC) contract, clear both dates that are reflected in red under the Proj/Act field and press tab. Confirm that the dates are cleared. [image] Ensure that the contract status is &lsquo;Delivered' and click Save. [image] If the contract is in &lsquo;Redelivered', please change the status accordingly. Otherwise, you will face the following error: [image] Expand the voyage tab on the right of the page and navigate to the first voyage for the TC contract. Click save. [image] If there is only one voyage for the TC contract, proceed to that specific voyage. [image] Return to the TC contract (If the TC contract is already open in another tab, ensure that the page is refreshed to see the changes). Check that the Delivery Proj/Act fields are no longer reflected in red. [image] If there is only one voyage, clicking save on the voyage will update both the Delivery or Redelivery Proj/Act fields. No further action is required. [image] Navigate to the last voyage of the TC contract and click save. Note* Ensure that either 'Last TCO voyage' or 'Last TCI voyage' is checked. [image] If it is a TCO voyage, ensure a Redelivery port (Z) is entered. [image] Once saved, return to the TC contract. (If the TC contract is already open in another tab, ensure that the page is refreshed to see the changes.) [image] Only the Delivery or Redelivery Proj/Act fields are reflected in red: In the Time charter (TC) contract, clear the date that is reflected in red under the Proj/Act field and press tab. Confirm that the date is cleared. If only the Delivery Date is cleared After clearing the date, click Save. [image] Go to the voyage where the vessel was delivered in this contract and click save. If there are multiple voyages, go to the first one in the contract. Once saved, return to the TC contract. (If the TC contract is already open in another tab, ensure that the page is refreshed to see the changes.) If only the Redelivery Date is cleared: After clearing the date, ensure that the TC contract status is updated to &lsquo;Delivered'; otherwise, you will be faced with the following error. [image] Go to the voyage where the vessel was redelivered in this contract and click save. If there are multiple voyages, go to the last one in the contract. Once saved, return to the TC contract. (If the TC contract is already open in another tab, ensure that the page is refreshed to see the changes.)\"},{\"id\":\"1196917108\",\"title\":\"Why does my Time Charter Hire amount seem incorrect?\",\"text\":\"There are many variables the come into play regarding the time charter amount displayed within the time charter bill. [image] The reason why the Time Charter Bill displays a different amount than the \\\"expected\\\" Monthly rate set within the contract is due to the number of days in the previous month (February 28 days) and the start of the period (12:45). Having less days in the previous month creates a higher generalized rate for the month of February compared to March. Therefore, the remaining 11.25 hours or .46875 days of February's Hire will be greater than the 11.25 hours or .46875 days missing from the Monthly hire of March. 1062500/28= 37946.42857*.46875= 17787.38839 1062500/31= 34274.19355*.46875= 16066.02823 17787.38839-16066.02823= 1721.36 1064221.36-1,062,500.00= 1721.36\"},{\"id\":\"695468157\",\"title\":\"Why is there no voyage number in Time Charter Commission Payment line item?\",\"text\":\"You may encounter a situation whereby the voyage number is not reflected on the Time Charter Commission Payment screen when creating a commission invoice. One of the possible reasons is because you may already have an Incremental Hire invoice raised ahead of the hire commission. In such cases, the voyage number will not be displayed in the Time Charter Commission Payment line item. If you would like for the voyage number to be displayed, You will first have to delete the Incremental Hire invoice, Raise the commission invoice, Then raise the Incremental Hire invoice at the end of the Time Charter period. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"737116311\",\"title\":\"Why Period From date in TCO Bill is not based on Proj/Act GMT delivery date in TCO contract?\",\"text\":\"Scenario: In a TCOV or TCTO voyage, if the Operator needs to create the first hire or bill invoice before the vessel's actual delivery date, they need to take the following into consideration. Before we dive into the steps taken for the first hire invoice under this condition, let's observe the fields \\\"Est GMT\\\" (estimated time of delivery )and \\\"Proj/Act GMT\\\" (projected/actual time of delivery). The field \\\"Est GMT\\\" is manually filled in by the creator of the TC contract, whereas the field \\\"Proj/Act GMT\\\" is automatically populated by the latest voyage existing in IMOS. For example, if the itinerary of the latest current voyage in IMOS gives an estimated time of voyage completion \\\"09/01/20 01.42\\\", then this date will be auto-populated in \\\"Proj/Act GMT\\\" in blue fonts, which indicates that this date is projected time of delivery. When the new voyage under the TC contract you have created \\\"commences\\\" (Status: Commenced), then the filed \\\"Proj/Act GMT\\\" will be automatically updated in black fonts, which means that the delivery date is now actual . If there is no previous voyage in the system, then Est GMT with coincide with Proj/Act GMT. For this scenario, the Operator needs to prepare the hire/ bill invoice before the delivery of the vessel. Since the vessel has not arrived at the delivery port yet and the voyage status is \\\" scheduled \\\", notice that the status of the TCO contract is fixed . [image] At this stage when you raise the TCO bill, the \\\" Period From\\\" date of the invoice displays the Est GMT date of the TCO contact. [image] When the vessel has arrived at the delivery port, the status of the TCO contract is automatically updated from Fixed to Delivered , and the \\\"Period From\\\" date will show the Proj/Actua l Delivery date of the TCO contact. [image] &zwnj; &zwnj;\"},{\"id\":\"714113206\",\"title\":\"Will the \\\"To GMT\\\" field for a hire rate in the Pricing grid of a Time Charter contract be extended automatically if the voyage redelivery is extended?\",\"text\":\"For Time Charter contracts , only the Delivery and Redelivery Date and Time are driven by the voyage if they are properly linked. For more information on this, see How do I relink a Time Charter contract to a voyage? In the event that the redelivery date extends beyond the Hire Period specified in the Pricing grid, the system will not automatically update To GMT to reflect the redelivery date. This will have to be manually updated or added. [image]\"}]}"